      "title": "Monte Carlo simulation in Python: estimating the probability of at least one 6 in 20 die rolls",
      "url": "https://www.statology.org/python-monte-carlo-simulation-dice/",
      "snippet": "This tutorial shows how to simulate 1,000,000 trials in NumPy using np.random.randint(1, 7, size=(1000000, 20)). It notes that the theoretical probability of at least one 6 in 20 rolls is 1 - (5/6)^20 \u2248 0.9739.",
      "domain": "statology.org",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Python Dice Rolling Simulation with NumPy and Monte Carlo Methods",
      "url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-dice-rolling-simulation/",
      "snippet": "GeeksforGeeks demonstrates generating random integers from 1 to 6 with NumPy and counting trials where at least one roll equals 6. The article compares simulated results from 100,000 and 1,000,000 trials to the exact probability formula 1 - (5/6)^n.",
      "domain": "geeksforgeeks.org",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "How to Simulate Dice Rolls in Python Using NumPy Random",
      "url": "https://realpython.com/numpy-random-number-generator/",
      "snippet": "Real Python explains how to use numpy.random.default_rng().integers to create large batches of die rolls for Monte Carlo experiments. In one example, 1 million simulated trials are used to estimate the chance of rolling at least one 6, with results converging near the analytic probability as trial count increases.",
      "domain": "realpython.com",
      "rank": 3
    },
    {
      "title": "Probability of at Least One Six in Repeated Dice Rolls",
      "url": "https://www.mathsisfun.com/data/probability-events.html",
      "snippet": "Math is Fun gives the complement-rule calculation for repeated die rolls: the chance of no 6 in n rolls is (5/6)^n, so the chance of at least one 6 is 1 - (5/6)^n. For 10 rolls, the result is about 83.8%, and for 20 rolls it rises to about 97.4%.",
      "domain": "mathsisfun.com",
      "rank": 4
    },
    {
      "title": "NumPy Random Sampling and Monte Carlo Simulation Examples",
      "url": "https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/generated/numpy.random.Generator.integers.html",
      "snippet": "The NumPy documentation for Generator.integers describes sampling integer values over a half-open interval, such as generating die faces from 1 to 7 with endpoint excluded. The examples section shows how to use the Generator API introduced in NumPy 1.17 for reproducible simulations with large sample sizes like 1,000,000 draws.",
      "domain": "numpy.org",
      "rank": 5
    }
  ],
  "query": "Python 1 million trials die rolls simulation probability at least one 6 numpy",
  "count": 5
}

