    {
      "title": "Coefficient of Variation (CV) - Statistics By Jim",
      "url": "https://statisticsbyjim.com/basics/coefficient-variation/",
      "snippet": "The coefficient of variation is calculated as standard deviation divided by the mean, often multiplied by 100 to express it as a percentage. For example, a data set with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 5 has a CV of 10%.",
      "domain": "statisticsbyjim.com",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Relative Standard Deviation (RSD) and Coefficient of Variation",
      "url": "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman2/auxillar/rsd.htm",
      "snippet": "NIST defines the relative standard deviation as 100 \u00d7 s / x\u0304, where s is the sample standard deviation and x\u0304 is the sample mean. The same measure is commonly called the coefficient of variation and is used to compare variability across datasets with different units or means.",
      "domain": "nist.gov",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "Standard Deviation and Variance - Khan Academy",
      "url": "https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability/summarizing-quantitative-data/variance-standard-deviation/a/standard-deviation-review",
      "snippet": "For a population, the standard deviation is the square root of the variance, and the variance is the average of squared deviations from the mean. A small example with values 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 has a mean of 5 and a population standard deviation of 2.",
      "domain": "khanacademy.org",
      "rank": 3
    },
    {
      "title": "Coefficient of Variation Calculator and Formula",
      "url": "https://www.calculatorsoup.com/calculators/statistics/coefficient-variation.php",
      "snippet": "Coefficient of variation is a standardized measure of dispersion calculated by dividing the standard deviation by the mean. If the mean is 25 and the standard deviation is 3.5, the coefficient of variation is 0.14, or 14%.",
      "domain": "calculatorsoup.com",
      "rank": 4
    },
    {
      "title": "Population Standard Deviation Formula - Math is Fun",
      "url": "https://www.mathsisfun.com/data/standard-deviation-formulas.html",
      "snippet": "The population standard deviation formula uses N in the denominator and is written as \u03c3 = \u221a[\u03a3(x\u2212\u03bc)\u00b2 / N]. In a simple data set of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, the mean is 3 and the population standard deviation is about 1.41.",
      "domain": "mathsisfun.com",
      "rank": 5
    }
  ],
  "query": "population standard deviation coefficient of variation formula mean dataset",
  "count": 5
}

