    {
      "title": "Flu and People 65 Years and Older",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/highrisk/65over.htm",
      "snippet": "Adults 65 years and older are at higher risk of serious flu complications, including hospitalization and death. CDC notes that a preferential recommendation exists for higher-dose, adjuvanted, and recombinant flu vaccines for this age group during the 2024-2025 flu season.",
      "domain": "cdc.gov",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "ACIP Recommendations Summary | Influenza Vaccine Use, 2024-2025",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/hcp/acip/index.htm",
      "snippet": "For the 2024-2025 season, CDC\u2019s Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices recommends annual influenza vaccination for everyone 6 months and older. For adults 65 years and older, CDC says any age-appropriate flu vaccine is acceptable, but higher-dose, adjuvanted, or recombinant vaccines are preferred when available.",
      "domain": "cdc.gov",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "The Best Time to Get a Flu Vaccine",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/prevent/vaccinations.htm",
      "snippet": "CDC says September and October are generally the best months to get vaccinated, especially for adults 65 years and older. The page also notes that antibody levels usually take about 2 weeks to develop after vaccination and that flu vaccination can reduce illness, doctor visits, and hospitalizations.",
      "domain": "cdc.gov",
      "rank": 3
    },
    {
      "title": "What Older Adults Need to Know About Flu Vaccine Options",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/prevent/older-adults.htm",
      "snippet": "Older adults have a greater risk of flu-related complications because immune response declines with age, a process known as immunosenescence. CDC explains that higher-dose, adjuvanted, and recombinant influenza vaccines are designed to help improve protection for people 65 and older.",
      "domain": "cdc.gov",
      "rank": 4
    },
    {
      "title": "2024-2025 Flu Season: Vaccine Options for Adults 65 Years and Older",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccine-types/older-adults-2024.htm",
      "snippet": "CDC guidance for the 2024-2025 season highlights three flu vaccine types for adults 65 and older: Fluzone High-Dose, Fluad (adjuvanted), and Flublok (recombinant). These products are used to improve immune response in older adults, who account for the majority of flu-related deaths each year.",
      "domain": "cdc.gov",
      "rank": 5
    }
  ],
