      "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/influenza-vaccination",
      "snippet": "WHO says influenza vaccination is recommended every year for pregnant women, children aged 6 months to 5 years, older adults, people with chronic medical conditions, and health workers. The organization notes that vaccine composition is updated annually and that protection can wane over time, which is why yearly vaccination is advised.",
      "domain": "who.int",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Influenza vaccines: WHO position paper \u2013 November 2024",
      "url": "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/who-wer-2024-xx",
      "snippet": "In its 2024 position paper, WHO reiterates that annual vaccination is recommended for older adults, pregnant women, health workers, and people with certain chronic conditions. The guidance references seasonal influenza vaccines and explains that the WHO vaccine composition recommendations are issued twice a year for the northern and southern hemispheres.",
      "domain": "who.int",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "Seasonal influenza: vaccination of older adults \u2013 WHO guidance",
      "url": "https://www.who.int/teams/immunization-vaccines-and-biologicals/diseases/influenza",
